  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Departing high pressure gives way to a cold \nfront and increasing shower and thunderstorm chances by \nThursday.\n\nHigh pressure moving overhead will maintain dry conditions with\npartly to mostly sunny skies today. The high will move offshore\ntonight as a cold front approaches from the Great Lakes. Clouds\nwill increase this evening, with shower chances ticking up near\nand west of I-81 after midnight. Shower activity is possible\nacross much of the area by daybreak Thursday, depending on the\nevolution of showers and storms over the OH Valley today.\n\nLatest guidance is a bit slower and further west/northwest with\nthe position of a cold front on Thursday. SLP progs hint at two\nsubtle warm front type features that will lift northeast during\nthe day on Thursday ahead of a pair of weak surface lows (one\nnear PA/NJ, and the other near central VA). Where this front\nends up just prior to peak heating will be crucial in\ndetermining the severe weather threat locally on Thursday.\n\nForecast soundings show strong mid-level capping west of the\nfront Thursday afternoon, with no capping and notably stronger\nCAPE approaching or exceeding 2000 J/kg east of it. The\nconsensus is for this front to be more or less over I-95\nThursday afternoon during peak heating, though there is some\nvariance anywhere from near the Blue Ridge to the Chesapeake\nBay. Mid-level lapse rates start the day unimpressive, around 5\nC/km likely due to saturation aloft associated with a weak wave\nof showers, but steepen slightly closer to 6 C/km by afternoon\ndue to some cooling aloft.\n\nThe extent of any clouds and showers cast some uncertainty on \nthe extent of heating and instability later in the day. The \nbackground environment is favorable for strong to severe \nupdrafts and even supercells. An 80-90+ kt upper jet will pivot \nover the area, with its right entrance ascent positioned over \nthe surface frontal zone in the afternoon. Mid-level heights \nstay relatively steady, but PVA (strongest north) will be \nprevalent ahead of the aforementioned mid/upper wave and jet.\n \nFlow will increase through the column, with 25-40 kts in the \n2-5 km AGL layer and effective deep layer bulk shear around 40 \nkts. SRH also increases to 100-200 m2/s2. Initial discrete cells\nin this environment may quickly grow upscale into a line \ncapable of surges of strong to severe straight-line wind and a \nfew embedded spin ups. Some hail can't be ruled out in the\nstrongest storms due to cooling temps aloft. Finally, although \nCorfidi vectors are not too low (10-15 kts), on the mesoscale \nsome backbuilding or training potential may exist on the tail \n(southwest) end of any developing line heading into the evening.\n\nAgain, the highest potential will be along and east of the front\nwhich currently appears to be near I-95 during the afternoon\ninto the early evening hours.\n\nAlthough somewhat conditional based on (1) the placement of the\nfront, and (2) the degree of instability, Thursday's severe\nweather and excessive rainfall risk bears monitoring. For now,\ngiven the uncertainties listed above, the risk is more in the\nMarginal (level 1 out of 5) bin for severe weather (i.e. any\ndamaging winds, large hail, tornadoes), but with a conditionally\nmuch higher ceiling than most typical late summer days. The \nexcessive rainfall/flood risk may be even more conditional due \nto uncertain mesoscale factors and lingering drought.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Chances for showers and thunderstorms continue \nthrough at least Saturday, before cooler and drier air moves in\nearly next week.\n\nThe longwave pattern across the CONUS will amplify moving into the \nweekend, with a shortwave disturbance and an even more potent\nupper-level low digging into the Ohio Valley and Great Lakes on\nSaturday. Downstream of the trough axis, we'll be in a \nfavorable location for large scale ascent, with showers and \nthunderstorms possible Saturday afternoon. The upper trough axis\nwill shift eastward to near our longitude by Sunday as an upper\nlow continues to deepen to the north of Lake Ontario. Most \nsolutions favor a cold front moving through either late Saturday\nnight or early Sunday. Drier low-level air will filter into the\nregion behind the front, with dewpoints dropping through the \n60s on Sunday. That combined with the trough axis being located \noverhead (leading to less large scale ascent), leads to most solutions\nshowing dry conditions locally. However, there still are a few \nsolutions that produce a passing shower or storm on Sunday.\n\nEven drier air will advect into the area for Monday and Tuesday, \nwith dewpoints forecast to drop into the 50s. This will lead to dry \nconditions, and also make it feel considerably less humid. High \ntemperatures each day Saturday through Tuesday will generally be in \nthe low to mid 80s at lower elevations, with 70s in the mountains.",
